<!-- §01 — The Dilemma -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Dilemma</div>
<h2>Bidirectional attention or KV cache — pick one</h2>
<p>Discrete diffusion language models (dLLMs) generate text by denoising masked sequences. At each of <em>T</em> steps, every masked position predicts its token based on what it can <em>see</em> — and the architectural choice of what it can see determines everything.</p>

<div class="attn-grid-wrap">
  <div class="attn-card">
    <div class="attn-card-title bidir">Bidirectional<span class="attn-tag good">Quality ✓</span><span class="attn-tag bad">Cache ✗</span></div>
    <canvas id="canvas-bidir" width="160" height="100" style="display:block;margin:0 auto;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div class="attn-desc">Every position attends to every other. Full context, maximum quality — but each token's KV depends on unresolved masked tokens, so the prefix KV cache is invalid. Every denoising step recomputes the entire attention matrix. Throughput collapses at long prompts: 483 → 53 tok/s as P grows from 512 → 4096.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="attn-card">
    <div class="attn-card-title causal">Causal<span class="attn-tag good">Cache ✓</span><span class="attn-tag bad">Right ctx ✗</span></div>
    <canvas id="canvas-causal" width="160" height="100" style="display:block;margin:0 auto;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div class="attn-desc">Each position only sees tokens to its left. Prefix KV is computed once and reused across all T denoising steps. Fast — but structurally blind to the right side. Every masked position loses the information-theoretic "right-context correction" ∆<sup>R2L</sup>.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="attn-card">
    <div class="attn-card-title bifocal">Bifocal (R2LM)<span class="attn-tag good">Cache ✓</span><span class="attn-tag good">Right ctx ✓</span></div>
    <canvas id="canvas-bifocal" width="160" height="100" style="display:block;margin:0 auto;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div class="attn-desc">Causal attention (exact left context, KV cached) <em>plus</em> a reverse Mamba sidecar (compressed right context, no cache invalidation). The attention mask stays strictly causal. Right-side information arrives via a non-attention pathway — so the cache stays valid.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The key insight from the paper's information-theoretic analysis: the quality gap between causal and bidirectional dLLMs equals the <strong>conditional mutual information of each masked token with the right-side observed tokens</strong>. This gap grows with how many tokens lie to the right and is largest for early positions in a long sequence. You can't recover this information with tricks on the causal side — you need a pathway that actually looks right.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 — Background -->
<section id="s-background" data-code="background">
<div class="section-no">02 — dLLM Background</div>
<h2>How masked diffusion works</h2>
<p>A masked diffusion language model (MDLM) corrupts a sequence by independently replacing each token with a <code>[MASK]</code> at rate <em>1 − α<sub>t</sub></em> for timestep <em>t ∈ [0, 1]</em>. The forward process uses a linear schedule α<sub>t</sub> = 1 − t: at t=0 the sequence is fully clean; at t=1 fully masked.</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">MDLM Training Objective</div>
<span class="math-var">L</span>(θ) = E<sub>t,x,z_t</sub> w(t) · Σ<sub>i∈M_t</sub> [−log p<sub>θ</sub>(<span class="math-var">x</span><sub>i</sub> | <span class="math-var">z</span><sub>t</sub>)]
<br>
w(t) = 1/(1−t) <span class="math-comment">  ← upweights high-mask-rate steps</span>
<br><br>
M<sub>t</sub> = masked positions at time t
<br>
O<sub>t</sub> = observed (unmasked) positions at time t
</div>

<p>At inference, generation starts fully masked and runs T denoising steps (typically T=32). At each step, the model assigns confidence scores to every masked position and unmasks the top fraction — the positions with highest max-softmax probability.</p>

<h3>The right-context gap — a worked example</h3>
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">right-context-gap.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">watch a position decode with and without right context</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="chart-controls">
      <button class="chart-btn active" id="btn-step-0" onclick="setGapStep(0)">Position 3</button>
      <button class="chart-btn" id="btn-step-1" onclick="setGapStep(1)">Position 5</button>
      <button class="chart-btn" id="btn-step-2" onclick="setGapStep(2)">Position 7</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="gap-canvas" width="580" height="160" style="width:100%;max-width:580px;border-radius:8px;display:block"></canvas>
    <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem">The token <strong>"mat"</strong> (position 3) is being denoised. Causal attention only sees "The cat sat on the" (left). Right context "." provides crucial disambiguation — without it, "mat" competes equally with "chair", "floor", etc.</p>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §03 — Architecture -->
<section id="s-architecture" data-code="architecture">
<div class="section-no">03 — R2LM Architecture</div>
<h2>A causal backbone with a reverse sidecar</h2>
<p>R2LM attaches a reverse Mamba SSM as a residual stream to a pretrained autoregressive Transformer. The key design constraint: <strong>the attention mechanism stays strictly causal</strong>. Right-context information arrives only through the SSM pathway, which does not consume or invalidate causal KV cache entries.</p>

<div class="vp">
