<h3>Two training modes</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Joint mode</strong>: train backbone + R2L stream together from an AR checkpoint. The zero-initialized gate means the model starts identical to the causal dLLM and gradually opens the right-context pathway.</li>
  <li><strong>Plug-in mode (R2LM-PI)</strong>: freeze the backbone, train only the R2L stream (185M params) on top of an already-trained causal dLLM checkpoint. Just 5B tokens (1/12th of joint training) — and it posts the highest overall average.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<!-- §04 — The R2L Stream -->
<section id="s-mechanism" data-code="mechanism">
<div class="section-no">04 — The R2L Stream</div>
<h2>Four steps: flip, scan, unflip, gate</h2>
<p>The R2L stream is the minimal mechanism that supplies position-aware right context. Every step has a clear purpose — and removing or wrong-directing any step makes things worse (confirmed by ablation).</p>

<div class="step-list" id="r2l-steps">
  <div class="step-box active" id="step-0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Sequence Flip</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Reverse the sequence axis: <code>h_flip = flip(h, dim=1)</code>. After flipping, position L−i−1 in h_flip corresponds to position i in the original sequence. A left-to-right Mamba scan on the flipped sequence now aggregates tokens from position i+1, i+2, … L — the right side.</div>
      <div class="step-code">h_flip = h.flip(dim=1)  # shape: [B, L, d]</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Mamba Selective Scan</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Run a Mamba-1 selective SSM block on the flipped sequence. Mamba's input-dependent transitions let it selectively gate which right-side tokens to retain. Critically, the SSM scan does not consume causal-attention KV entries — the cache stays valid.</div>
      <div class="step-code">h_mamba = MambaBlock(h_flip)  # linear time O(BLd_ssm)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Unflip + LayerNorm</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Flip back: <code>h_R2L = LN(h_mamba.flip(dim=1))</code>. After unflipping, position i in h_R2L summarizes tokens at j ≥ i — the right-context correction. LayerNorm bounds the magnitude before the residual is added.</div>
      <div class="step-code">h_r2l = LN(h_mamba.flip(dim=1))  # back to original order</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-3">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Gated Residual Addition</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Add h_R2L to the backbone hidden state via a per-layer scalar gate: <code>h⁺ = h + h_R2L · tanh(s)</code>. The scalar s is initialised to 0 — the augmented model is bit-identical to the causal baseline at step 0. tanh saturation bounds the residual at |tanh(s)| ≤ 1.</div>
      <div class="step-code">h_out = h + h_r2l * torch.tanh(self.gate)  # s=0 at init</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
  <span class="step-prog" id="step-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
</div>

<h3>Why direction matters — the ablation</h3>
<p>The paper tests three parameter-matched controls on 1B tokens to rule out "it's just more parameters":</p>
<table class="results-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Variant</th><th>Extra Params</th><th>Train Loss ↓</th><th>WikiText-103 PPL ↓</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Causal dLLM (baseline)</td><td class="mn">0</td><td class="mn">4.66</td><td class="mn">178</td></tr>
    <tr><td>+ MLP Adapter (no direction)</td><td class="mn">185M</td><td class="mn">4.63</td><td class="mn" style="color:#f87171">356 ↑worse</td></tr>
    <tr><td>+ L2R Mamba (wrong direction)</td><td class="mn">185M</td><td class="mn">4.84</td><td class="mn" style="color:#f87171">374 ↑worse</td></tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row"><td>+ R2L Mamba (R2LM, ours)</td><td class="mn">185M</td><td class="mn best">3.18</td><td class="mn best">42</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Bidirectional dLLM (upper bound)</td><td class="mn">0</td><td class="mn">3.02</td><td class="mn">30</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The MLP adapter adds parameters without direction — WikiText PPL doubles to 356. The L2R Mamba scans the same direction as causal attention (redundant, not novel) — it actively <em>worsens</em> training loss. Only the reversed scan recovers 90.2% of the causal-to-bidirectional training loss gap and reduces WikiText perplexity by 4.2×.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 — Throughput -->
<section id="s-throughput" data-code="throughput">
<div class="section-no">05 — Throughput</div>
<h2>12.9× the throughput of bidirectional dLLMs</h2>
<p>The throughput advantage of KV caching compounds with prompt length. As <em>P</em> grows, the bidirectional dLLM must recompute the full (P+G)² attention at every denoising step — throughput collapses. R2LM's causal backbone reads from cache; only the R2L scan cost grows (with G, not P).</p>

