<h3>Plug-in is surprisingly strong</h3>
<p>R2LM-PI trains on a <em>frozen</em> causal backbone for only 5B tokens (vs 60B for joint) updating only 185M parameters (vs 1.85B for joint). Yet it posts the highest ALL average (47.76%) and the highest Short average (52.15%). This isolates the value of the R2L stream from joint backbone training and provides a recipe for retrofitting any existing causal dLLM with right-context access.</p>

<!-- Listen-friendly content -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display:none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: Bifocal dLLMs — Right Context Without Breaking the Cache</h2>
    <p>Discrete diffusion language models denoise masked sequences in parallel, offering big speedups over autoregressive generation. But they face a hard architectural dilemma: bidirectional attention gives full context but invalidates the KV cache, forcing expensive full recomputation at every denoising step; causal attention keeps the cache but loses all right-side context, hurting quality.</p>
    <p>R2LM, the Bifocal dLLM instantiation from Meta AI and UNC Chapel Hill, resolves this by keeping causal attention strictly causal while adding a reverse Mamba SSM sidecar that injects compressed right-context information without touching the cache. The four-step R2L stream flips the sequence, runs a Mamba selective scan, unflips it, then adds the result back via a zero-initialized gated residual. At initialization the model is bit-identical to the causal baseline; as training proceeds, the gate opens and the right-context pathway contributes. Ablations confirm the direction matters: an MLP adapter (no direction) doubles perplexity, and an L2R Mamba (wrong direction) makes things worse. Only the reversed scan recovers 90.2% of the causal-to-bidirectional training loss gap. In throughput, R2LM is 2.4 to 12.9 times faster than bidirectional dLLMs (the gap widens with prompt length) while staying within 7 to 16 percent of the causal baseline's speed. On seven multiple-choice benchmarks after 60B tokens of continued pretraining on Qwen3-1.7B, R2LM averages 47.71 percent — exceeding both the causal baseline at 44.60 and the bidirectional dLLM at 46.74. The plug-in variant, trained for only 5B tokens on a frozen backbone, posts the highest overall average at 47.76 percent.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：Bifocal dLLM——无需破坏缓存获取右侧上下文</h2>
    <p>离散扩散语言模型通过并行去噪掩码序列来生成文本，相比自回归生成具有显著的速度优势。但这类模型面临一个严峻的架构难题：双向注意力能提供完整上下文，但会使KV缓存失效，导致每次去噪步骤都需要重新计算全部注意力；因果注意力保留了缓存，但失去了所有右侧上下文信息，影响生成质量。</p>
    <p>来自Meta AI和北卡罗来纳大学教堂山分校的R2LM（Bifocal dLLM实例）通过保持因果注意力严格单向，同时添加一个反向Mamba SSM辅助模块来注入压缩的右侧上下文信息，而不影响缓存的有效性。四步R2L流程包括：翻转序列、运行Mamba选择性扫描、再翻转回来，最后通过零初始化的门控残差连接添加结果。消融实验确认方向至关重要：无方向性的MLP适配器会使困惑度翻倍，而正向Mamba则使情况更糟。只有反向扫描才能恢复90.2%的因果到双向训练损失差距。在吞吐量方面，R2LM比双向dLLM快2.4到12.9倍（差距随提示长度增大），同时与因果基线速度相差不超过7到16%。在7个多项选择基准测试中，R2LM平均得分47.71%，超越因果基线的44.60%和双向dLLM的46.74%。</p>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

